    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1245">

                <pb facs="1135" n="877" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg877"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72111/2234</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of the Navy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Daniels</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the</hi>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Secretary of State</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-05-24">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 24, 1915</hi>
                        </date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to acknowledge the receipt of your
                    letter of May 15, in which you inquire whether the action of Commander Palmer of
                    the <hi rendition="#italic">Iroquois</hi> in communicating with the shore at the
                    port of Martinique and arranging for signals as to the movements of the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Sumter</hi>, as indicated in his reports, received the
                    approval or disapproval of the Navy Department at the time.</p>
                <p>A careful search of the records in the department fails to show that the
                    department expressed either approval or disapproval of his action.</p>
                <p>There are enclosed herewith copies of orders to Commander Palmer,<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d1245-f1"/> which show that shortly after the incident in question
                    he was ordered home in order that a full inquiry might be conducted in reference
                    to the incidents connected with the escape of the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Sumter</hi>. The records of the proceedings of this inquiry are,
                    unfortunately, not on file.</p>
                <p>Very sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Josephus Daniels</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
